/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take the air in a closed room.
   - The macroscopic approach (also called the classical view) treats the same air as a body with a handful of measurable properties: pressure p, volume V, temperature T, mass m and density ρ.
   - The two are not rival theories.
   - The link between the two views is an average.
   - For a gas at ordinary conditions the number of molecules per cubic metre follows from p, T and the Boltzmann constant:
   - n is the number density in molecules per m³; p is absolute pressure in Pa; k = 1.380649 × 10⁻²³ J/K is the Boltzmann constant; T is absolute temperature in K.
   - Between collisions a molecule travels on average a distance called the mean free path λ.
   - λ is the mean free path in m; d is the effective molecular diameter in m; k, T and p as above.
   - Density at a point is defined by shrinking a small volume δV around the point and dividing the mass inside by the volume: ρ = lim δm/δV as δV shrinks towards δV′, the smallest volume that still holds enough molecules for a steady average.
   - The size of the scatter follows from statistics: a cube holding N molecules on average gives a count that varies by about √N, a relative spread of 1/√N.
*/
