/* book exam traps: Pressure is caused by molecular impacts, but it is a macroscopic property: a gauge reads the average of an enormous number of impacts, never one molecule. | The continuum is not about the size of the object alone. A 10 mm tube is a continuum at 101 kPa (Kn ≈ 7 × 10⁻⁶) but not at 1 Pa (Kn ≈ 0.7): pressure matters through λ. | Use absolute temperature in K and absolute pressure in Pa in n = p/(kT) and λ = kT/(√2 π d² p). Writing 27 °C for T gives an answer eleven times too large. | Do not say the two approaches give different laws. They describe the same state; the macroscopic laws can be derived from the microscopic model, and they agree. */
