/* book: lead: Every thermodynamics problem starts by drawing a boundary. The system is what you choose to study; everything outside that can interact with it is the surroundings; the boundary is the surface, real or imaginary, fixed or moving, that separates them. What crosses the boundary decides the type of system: a closed system lets energy cross but no mass, an open system (control volume) lets both cross, */
/* book Figure 1.2.1: The worked example’s rigid tank seen with two boundaries. Drawn around the tank (A), the boundary is fixed and 0.50 kg of air crosses it, so the system is open. Drawn around the 1.5 kg that stays (B), no mass crosses, so it is a closed system whose boundary moves. */
