/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Split a system in equilibrium into two equal halves.
   - v is specific volume in m³/kg; V is volume in m³; m is mass in kg; ρ is density in kg/m³.
   - A property depends only on the state, never on how the system got there.
   - How many properties fix a state?
   - Two properties are independent when one can change while the other is held constant.
   - An equation of state is a formula that links p, v and T.
   - p is absolute pressure in kPa; v is specific volume in m³/kg; T is absolute temperature in K; R is the gas constant in kJ/(kg·K); R̄ = 8.314 kJ/(kmol·K) is the universal gas constant; M is the molar mass in kg/kmol.
   - Given any two of p, v and T, the equation gives the third: the state postulate at work.
   - Because two properties fix the state, a state can be drawn as a point on a graph of any two properties: a state diagram.
   - A process made of equilibrium states, a quasi-static process, appears as a continuous line between two state points.
*/
